## TEST 1 — Wrong DNS Server

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Fault type** | DNS misconfiguration |
| **Layer** | DNS (Layer 7 — Name Resolution) |
| **Who acts** | Mac 2 (Aditya) |
| **Fault** | DNS resolver changed from `10.7.15.236` to `10.7.99.99` |
| **Reversible** | Yes — one command to restore |

### Initial State
- DNS server: `10.7.15.236` (Om / dnsmasq)
- `nslookup app.team1.test` → `10.7.17.151` ✅

### Fault Injection
```zsh
sudo networksetup -setdnsservers Wi-Fi 10.7.99.99
sudo dscacheutil -flushcache && sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder
```

### Test Command
```zsh
curl -v --cacert configs/tls/team1.test.crt https://app.team1.test:8443/api/status
```

### Observed Output
```
[FILL from evidence/script8/test1_wrong_dns/test1_output.txt]
```

### Expected Output
```
* Could not resolve host: app.team1.test
curl: (6) Could not resolve host: app.team1.test
```

### Layer Analysis
| Layer | Status | Observation |
|-------|--------|-------------|
| DNS | ❌ FAILED | Query sent to 10.7.99.99 — no response / timeout |
| TCP | ✖ Never reached | No IP to connect to |
| TLS | ✖ Never reached | — |
| Application | ✖ Never reached | — |

### Cause
The DNS resolver at `10.7.99.99` does not exist on this LAN. The query times out with no response. Without resolving `app.team1.test` to an IP, curl cannot form a TCP connection — all subsequent layers are unreachable.

### Restore
```zsh
sudo networksetup -setdnsservers Wi-Fi 10.7.15.236
sudo dscacheutil -flushcache && sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder
```

**Restored:** [ ] Confirmed — `nslookup app.team1.test` returns `10.7.17.151`

**Evidence file:** `evidence/script8/test1_wrong_dns/test1_output.txt`

---

## TEST 2 — Wrong DNS Record

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Fault type** | DNS wrong A record |
| **Layer** | DNS causes TCP failure |
| **Who acts** | Mac 1 — Om (edits dnsmasq.conf) |
| **Fault** | A record changed from `10.7.17.151` to `10.7.99.99` |
| **Reversible** | Yes — Om reverts dnsmasq.conf |

### Initial State
- dnsmasq A record: `app.team1.test → 10.7.17.151`

### Fault Injection (Mac 1 — Om)
Edit `/opt/homebrew/etc/dnsmasq.conf`:
```
# Change:  address=/app.team1.test/10.7.17.151
# To:      address=/app.team1.test/10.7.99.99
```
Then: `sudo brew services restart dnsmasq`

Mac 2 flush: `sudo dscacheutil -flushcache && sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder`

### Test Command (Mac 2)
```zsh
curl -v --cacert configs/tls/team1.test.crt https://app.team1.test:8443/api/status
```

### Observed Output
```
[FILL from evidence/script8/test2_wrong_record/test2_output.txt]
```

### Expected Output
```
* Trying 10.7.99.99:8443...
* connect to 10.7.99.99 port 8443 failed: Operation timed out
curl: (28) Failed to connect to app.team1.test port 8443 after ...ms
```

### Layer Analysis
| Layer | Status | Observation |
|-------|--------|-------------|
| DNS | ⚠ Responded | Returned wrong IP: `10.7.99.99` |
| TCP | ❌ FAILED | SYN sent to `10.7.99.99:8443` — no SYN-ACK, timeout |
| TLS | ✖ Never reached | — |
| Application | ✖ Never reached | — |

### Cause
DNS resolved successfully but returned the wrong IP. The TCP SYN is sent to `10.7.99.99` which has no host responding on this LAN. The connection times out waiting for SYN-ACK. The DNS layer did its job — but with incorrect data.

### Restore (Mac 1 — Om)
Revert dnsmasq.conf back to `address=/app.team1.test/10.7.17.151`, restart dnsmasq.
Mac 2: `sudo dscacheutil -flushcache && sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder`

**Restored:** [ ] Confirmed — `nslookup app.team1.test` returns `10.7.17.151`

**Evidence file:** `evidence/script8/test2_wrong_record/test2_output.txt`

