## TEST 5 — Stop Both Backends

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Fault type** | Total backend failure |
| **Layer** | Application (nginx — no upstream available) |
| **Who acts** | Mac 3 (Krishiv) + Mac 4 (Vaidehi) both stop backends |
| **Fault** | Both 10.7.10.0:3001 and 10.7.16.36:3002 stopped |
| **Reversible** | Yes — both restart their processes |

### Observed Output
```
[FILL from evidence/script8/test5_stop_both/test5_*.txt]
```

### Expected Output
```
HTTP/1.1 502 Bad Gateway
<html>
<head><title>502 Bad Gateway</title></head>
...
```

### Layer Analysis
| Layer | Status | Observation |
|-------|--------|-------------|
| DNS | ✅ OK | Resolves correctly |
| TCP (client→nginx) | ✅ OK | nginx accepts connection |
| TLS | ✅ OK | Handshake completes |
| Application (nginx→A) | ❌ FAILED | Connection refused |
| Application (nginx→B) | ❌ FAILED | Connection refused |
| nginx response | ❌ 502 | No upstream available |

### Cause
nginx has no healthy upstreams. It cannot proxy the request to any backend, so it returns `502 Bad Gateway` to the client. The TLS connection to nginx succeeds — the failure is purely at the application/upstream layer. The edge itself is still running.

### Restore
Both Krishiv and Vaidehi restart their backends.

**Restored:** [ ] Confirmed — round-robin A/B resumes, HTTP 200 returns

**Evidence file:** `evidence/script8/test5_stop_both/test5_*.txt`

