## TEST 6 — Wrong Destination Port

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Fault type** | TCP connection to wrong port |
| **Layer** | TCP |
| **Who acts** | Mac 2 (Aditya) — curl flag only |
| **Fault** | curl targets port 9999 instead of 8443 |
| **Reversible** | Yes — nothing changed, just a wrong curl flag |

### Initial State
- nginx listening on 8443 ✅
- Nothing listening on 9999

### Test Command
```zsh
curl -v --cacert configs/tls/team1.test.crt \
  --resolve app.team1.test:9999:10.7.17.151 \
  https://app.team1.test:9999/api/status 2>&1 | tee evidence/script8/test6_wrong_port/test6_output.txt
```

### Observed Output
```
[FILL from evidence/script8/test6_wrong_port/test6_output.txt]
```

### Expected Output
```
* Trying 10.7.17.151:9999...
* connect to 10.7.17.151 port 9999 failed: Connection refused
curl: (7) Failed to connect to app.team1.test port 9999 after 0 ms
```

### Layer Analysis
| Layer | Status | Observation |
|-------|--------|-------------|
| DNS | ✅ OK | Resolved (via --resolve flag) |
| TCP | ❌ FAILED | SYN to port 9999 → immediate RST (Connection refused) |
| TLS | ✖ Never reached | — |
| Application | ✖ Never reached | — |

### Cause
The IP is correct but port 9999 has no listener. The OS sends a TCP RST immediately upon receiving the SYN, resulting in instant `Connection refused`. Unlike Test 2 (timeout), this fails instantly because the host is reachable but actively refuses the connection on that port.

### Restore
Nothing to restore — no configuration was changed.

```zsh
# Verify system still healthy
curl -s --cacert configs/tls/team1.test.crt \
  --resolve app.team1.test:8443:10.7.17.151 \
  https://app.team1.test:8443/api/status
```

**Restored:** [ ] Confirmed — HTTP 200 with correct backend

**Evidence file:** `evidence/script8/test6_wrong_port/test6_output.txt`

